Here, \(q_{\mathrm{Langmuir}}\) is a Langmuir isotherm function (Equation 1(c)) with two adjustable parameters, \(K\) and \(Q_{\mathrm{m}}\), characterizing the initial slope and limiting sorption amount, respectively, at pressures below the np \(\leftrightarrow\) lp phase transition. The function \(q_{\mathrm{LJMY}}\) (Equation 1(b)) describes the stepped part of the sorption isotherm using three additional adjustable parameters, \(p_{\mathrm{tr}},\ \sigma,\) and \(Q_{\mathrm{step}}\), where the latter represents the increase in capacity after the phase transition (above that of \(q_{\mathrm{Langmuir}}\)). As detailed in Note 1 of the SI, the LJMY model can be derived by assuming the sorption site energies made available by the MOF's structural phase transition have a Gaussian distribution. It is analogous to the LJM model developed by Li et al.\(^{24}\), which describes the temperature-regulated admission and release of gases in microporous trapdoor materials. To describe a branch of a hysteretic stepped sorption
isotherm for a given MOF, the LJMY model may be combined with any classical sorption isotherm model (e.g., Sips and Toth), as required to best describe the data.
Figure 2 shows that the width of the MOF's structural hysteresis, \(\delta p_{\mathrm{tr}},\) decreases with reducing temperature. This is opposite to the behavior observed for hysteresis associated with capillary condensation in classical adsorbents with a rigid structure, which becomes larger at lower temperatures25,26.
Simple extrapolations of the apparently linear trends in \(p_{\mathrm{tr}}^{(\mathrm{ads})}\) and \(p_{\mathrm{tr}}^{(\mathrm{des})}\) with temperature exhibited in Figure 2 suggest that the hysteresis in the sorption isotherms might disappear (e.g., \(\delta p_{\mathrm{tr}}\rightarrow\) 0) around 210 and \(230\,\mathrm{K}\) for Fe(bdp) and Co(bdp), respectively. Measuring sorption capacities accurately at such temperatures is very challenging and so an alternative flexible adsorbent was sought to investigate the asymptotic behavior of structural hysteresis with temperature. Analysis of the data published by Arami-Niya et al.27 for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption on a zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-7) above \(273\,\mathrm{K}\) suggested that \(\delta p_{\mathrm{tr}}\rightarrow0\) as \(T\rightarrow\) \(265\,\mathrm{K},\) a significantly more accessible temperature.
